            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": ">I feel we should directly write a brand-new version with these rich features and share code with the current matchparen.lua. A progressive iteration might not be unnecessary. Once it matures, we can drop matchit.vim directly.\n\nQuote from #40664, here are explanations:\n\n1. At least one of our goals is to make `matchit.vim` work together with `matchparen`, so that the behavior becomes effectively WYSIWYG: `matchit` should jump to the position currently highlighted by `matchparen`. Therefore, we would need to trigger the search after a `CursorMoved` event, while pressing `%` should make `matchit` jump directly to a position that has already been computed.\n\n2. To perform searches on hot events such as `CursorMoved`, we need to avoid synchronous search. We therefore need to make the matching process async.\n\n3. If we intend to support tree-sitter-based matching, we will definitely need to replace `searchpair()`, since it is only based on regex.\n\nTherefore, it seems better to write a new implementation from scratch, which performs matching asynchronously within the event loop; `matchparen` would use the computed result for highlighting, while `matchit` would use the same result for navigation. The critical part is to write such a shared \"matching module\".\n\nJust to clarify, although I plan to continue to nvim over the next few weeks, I will mainly be focusing on other modules like json-rpc and logging. I would be very happy for anyone else to continue moving this work forward.\n",
